| --- | ---: | --- | --- |
| fresh build | 23.171 s | curve25519-sha256 / ssh-ed25519 / aes256-ctr / hmac-sha2-256 | auth 1; concurrent direct-tcpip 2; exec channels 7; rekeys 1; cleanup zero |
| unchanged binary 2 | 22.654 s | same | same counters; cleanup zero |
| unchanged binary 3 | 21.904 s | same | same counters; cleanup zero |

Each run exercises two concurrent direct channels to an authorized loopback
service, validates both SSH banners, runs a long-lived exec, uploads 48 KiB by
exec/stdin, completes a manual client rekey, cancels five exec/read operations,
then requires channels/socket/session/automatic tasks/custom allocations/
buffered bytes to equal zero.

The explicit environment gate is `RELUX_RUN_REAL_HOST_LIBSSH2=1`. Without it,
the ordinary suite emits `status=NOT_RUN reason=explicit-opt-in-absent`; an
unconfigured run is not represented as real-host evidence.

## Rekey range gap record

Exact open question left by the governing specs: **what admissible M0 numeric
envelope constrains the injected byte threshold, elapsed-time threshold, and
completion timeout before M3 selects production winners?**

The self-check covered `.spec/ssh-transport.md`, the conformance contract,
ADR-023, the supplied M3 evidence protocol, and downstream
`TASK-260715-1pn983`. They require injection, bounded failure, and later tuning,
but do not give an M0 envelope. ADR-014 now records:

- protected bytes per direction: 4 KiB–5 GiB inclusive;
- elapsed time: 100 ms–1 hour inclusive;
- completion timeout: the executed 10 s value only for M0 composition.

The lower byte/time probes execute the production automatic KEX path; the upper
byte/time values are preregistered M3 candidates. A wider timeout range is
explicitly unresolved and owned by `TASK-260715-1pn983` plus the M3 tuning/final
matrix. These are composition guardrails, not profile defaults or performance
claims. Out-of-scope checks confirmed that this adds no physical number, DNS
authorization, lane scheduling, relay behavior, UI, release approval, or NIOSSH
fork work.

